--- nuttx_audio_doc [1-135] ---
1: ===============
2: Audio Subsystem
3: ===============
4: 
5: This page discusses the audio subsystem support for NuttX which is only built if
6: ``CONFIG_AUDIO`` is defined in the NuttX configuration file.
7: 
8: Files in nuttx/audio
9: ====================
10: 
11: This directory holds the NuttX audio subsystem upper-half.  The upper-half provides
12: a common interface for applications to interface with and also defines a bind
13: layer for specific lower-half audio device drivers.
14: 
15: * ``audio.c`` - The upper-half driver that binds to a lower-half driver from the
16:   drivers/audio subdirectory.  For each attached audio device, there
17:   will be an instance of this upper-half driver bound to the
18:   instance of the lower half driver context.
19: * ``pcm_decode.c`` - Routines to decode PCM / WAV type data.
20: 
21: Portions of the audio system interface have application interfaces.  Those
22: portions reside in the ``nuttx/libc/audio`` directory where the will be built for
23: access by both OS driver logic and user application logic.  Those relevant
24: files in ``nuttx/libc/audio`` include:
25: 
26: * ``buffer.c``  - Routines to manage creattion and destruction of audio pipeline buffers
27:   (apb) used in the audio subsystem.  Audio pipeline buffers are passed
28:   between user applications and the audio drivers to deliver audio
29:   content for playback (or possibly recording in the future).
30: 
31: Related Header Files
32: ====================
33: 
34: ``include/nuttx/audio/audio.h`` - Top level include file defining the audio interface
35: ``include/nuttx/audio/vs1053.h`` - Specific driver initialization prototypes
36: 
37: Playback and Capture Flow
38: =========================
39: 
40: The upper half tracks a device-level state, held in ``struct audio_status_s``
41: and readable with ``AUDIOIOC_GETSTATUS``.  ``AUDIOIOC_START`` is only accepted
42: once the device has left ``AUDIO_STATE_OPEN``:
43: 
44: .. code-block:: none
45: 
46:    AUDIO_STATE_OPEN --configure--> AUDIO_STATE_PREPARED --start--> AUDIO_STATE_RUNNING
47:                                             ^                            |
48:                                             |                       pause | resume
49:                                             |                            v
50:                                        stop |                  AUDIO_STATE_PAUSED
51:                                             |
52:              AUDIO_STATE_DRAINING <----------+          (buffers under-run:
53:                      |                                  AUDIO_STATE_XRUN)
54:                      v
55:              AUDIO_STATE_OPEN  (once the lower half reports completion)
56: 
57: The states are defined in ``include/nuttx/audio/audio.h`` in ascending order of
58: priority, which is how the upper half aggregates the state of several
59: applications sharing one device:
60: 
61: ============================ ===== ===========================================
62: State                        Value Meaning
63: ============================ ===== ===========================================
64: ``AUDIO_STATE_OPEN``         0     Opened, no format established yet
65: ``AUDIO_STATE_PREPARED``     1     Format established, ready to be started
66: ``AUDIO_STATE_PAUSED``       2     Started, then paused
67: ``AUDIO_STATE_XRUN``         3     Started, but the application fell behind
68: ``AUDIO_STATE_DRAINING``     4     Stop requested, waiting for queued buffers
69: ``AUDIO_STATE_RUNNING``      5     Transferring
70: ============================ ===== ===========================================
71: 
72: A normal playback sequence is:
73: 
74: #. ``open()`` the device
75: #. ``AUDIOIOC_RESERVE`` to obtain a session (with ``CONFIG_AUDIO_MULTI_SESSION``)
76: #. ``AUDIOIOC_GETCAPS`` to query what the device supports
77: #. ``AUDIOIOC_CONFIGURE`` with ``ac_type`` set to ``AUDIO_TYPE_OUTPUT`` (or
78:    ``AUDIO_TYPE_INPUT``) to establish the stream format
79: #. ``AUDIOIOC_GETBUFFERINFO`` to learn the preferred buffer size and count
80: #. ``AUDIOIOC_ALLOCBUFFER`` for each buffer
81: #. ``AUDIOIOC_REGISTERMQ`` so the driver can report buffer completion
82: #. ``AUDIOIOC_ENQUEUEBUFFER`` to hand filled buffers to the driver
83: #. ``AUDIOIOC_START`` to begin transferring
84: #. ``AUDIOIOC_STOP`` when finished, then ``AUDIOIOC_FREEBUFFER`` and
85:    ``AUDIOIOC_RELEASE``
86: 
87: .. important::
88:    Step 4 is mandatory.  ``AUDIOIOC_CONFIGURE`` is the only thing that moves the
89:    device from ``AUDIO_STATE_OPEN`` to ``AUDIO_STATE_PREPARED``, so omitting it
90:    makes ``AUDIOIOC_START`` fail with ``-EPERM``.
91: 
92:    Note that a lower half configuring itself does not satisfy this: only a
93:    configuration arriving through the ioctl is observed by the upper half, so
94:    the state can still be ``AUDIO_STATE_OPEN`` while the hardware is already
95:    programmed.  The application must issue ``AUDIOIOC_CONFIGURE`` itself, with
96:    plausible non-zero values if it cannot determine the real ones.  Passing
97:    zeros is not a workaround - drivers reject a zero channel count or sample
98:    rate with ``-ERANGE`` or ``-EINVAL``, and some assert on it.
99: 
100: Buffer Ownership
101: ================
102: 
103: Audio pipeline buffers (``struct ap_buffer_s``, "apb") carry sample data
104: between the application and the lower-half driver.  ``AUDIOIOC_ALLOCBUFFER``
105: takes a ``struct audio_buf_desc_s`` and has two distinct modes, selected by the
106: ``u.pbuffer`` field:
107: 
108: **Private buffers** (``u.pbuffer != NULL``)
109: 
110:   The caller supplies the address of a pointer, and receives a buffer that it
111:   owns for its lifetime.  The upper half only forwards the request - to
112:   ``lower->ops->allocbuffer()`` if the driver implements it, otherwise to
113:   ``apb_alloc()``.  These buffers are enqueued individually by passing
114:   ``u.buffer`` to ``AUDIOIOC_ENQUEUEBUFFER``, and must be released with
115:   ``AUDIOIOC_FREEBUFFER``.  On success the ioctl returns
116:   ``sizeof(struct audio_buf_desc_s)``.
117: 
118:   This is the mode used by ``nxplayer``, ``nxrecorder`` and ``nxlooper``.
119: 
120: **Shared ring buffers** (``u.pbuffer == NULL``)
121: 
122:   The upper half allocates the buffer and keeps it in an internal ring, whose
123:   depth is bounded by the buffer count the lower half reported through
124:   ``AUDIOIOC_GETBUFFERINFO``.  Buffers are not handed back to the caller;
125:   instead the application enqueues by passing a descriptor with
126:   ``u.buffer == NULL``, and the upper half picks the next ring slot itself.
127:   This is what allows several applications to feed one device concurrently, and
128:   it is also the form that ``mmap()`` exposes.
129: 
130:   When the ring has already been populated - typically because another
131:   application attached first - ``AUDIOIOC_ALLOCBUFFER`` returns ``0`` rather
132:   than an error.  A second application is expected to treat that as success and
133:   proceed directly to ``AUDIOIOC_ENQUEUEBUFFER``.
134: 
135:   No in-tree application uses this mode yet; the upper half provides it so that
