../logs/pjjuihj/2026-08-15/claude-code__f7b6f300-fb20-405b-b863-157a4f8b0cce.jsonl:77:{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "f7b6f300-fb20-405b-b863-157a4f8b0cce", "team_id": "contest2026_329_dela", "github_login": "pjjuihj", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 76, "ts": "2026-08-15T06:19:23.939Z", "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "tokens_in": 3454, "tokens_out": 308, "role": "assistant", "text": "找到答案了！你的烧录指南里已经记录了这个问题：\n\n### SFBL 反复打印的原因和解决\n\n**原因**：AMOLED 屏拉低 USB 电源，导致供电不足，芯片不断复位。\n\n**解决方案**：改用 **5V/2A 充电头** 或 **电池供电**，不要用电脑 USB 口供电。\n\n来源：[flashing_guide.md](contest2026_329_dela/docs/flashing_guide.md) 第四节\"常见问题排查\"。\n\n---\n\n另外，正确的启动输出应该是：\n```\nSFBL\nABCD\nADC calibration data missing, use defaults\n...\nNuttShell (NSH)\nnsh>\n```\n\n如果只输出 `SFBL` 就卡住/重启，基本就是供电问题。后台 agent 还在研究更详细的 SFBL 恢复方案，完成后会通知你。"}
../logs/pjjuihj/2026-08-15/claude-code__f7b6f300-fb20-405b-b863-157a4f8b0cce.jsonl:78:{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "f7b6f300-fb20-405b-b863-157a4f8b0cce", "team_id": "contest2026_329_dela", "github_login": "pjjuihj", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 77, "ts": "2026-08-15T06:23:31.533Z", "role": "user", "text": "<task-notification>\n<task-id>a085733fef558aef6</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_1c2f3498c3a146e99fb5ecc1</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-cmj----1/f7b6f300-fb20-405b-b863-157a4f8b0cce/tasks/a085733fef558aef6.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Research SF32LB52 SFBL boot issue\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Research complete. Document written to `/home/cmj/桌面/1/docs/sf32lb52-sfbl-recovery.md`.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**What SFBL is:** SFBL is the ROM bootloader (mask ROM, immutable) in the SF32LB52 chip. It is NOT a software bootloader that can be corrupted. When the board prints \"SFBL\", the ROM bootloader has started and is attempting to validate and jump to the application at flash offset `0x12010000`.\n\n**Boot flow:** ROM bootloader (prints \"SFBL\") -&gt; validates vector table at `0x12010000` -&gt; jumps to NuttX `__start()` (prints \"ABCD\" during init) -&gt; NuttX kernel -&gt; NSH shell.\n\n**Root cause of the SFBL loop (from official docs):** The #1 documented cause on the HuangShan Pi is the **CO5300 AMOLED display pulling down the USB power rail**. The screen draws too much current for a laptop USB port, causing brownout during flash validation. The ROM bootloader then resets and retries in a loop.\n\n**Primary fix:** Use a **5V/2A USB charger** or battery power instead of a laptop USB port. If that doesn't work, disconnect the AMOLED FPC to confirm power is the issue.\n\n**Other causes documented:** corrupt application image (re-flash with sftool), wrong flash offset (must be `0x12010000`), serial tool holding RTS low (use `picocom --noreset --lower-rts`), and sftool connection timing issues (unplug/replug USB).\n\n**Architecture note:** The SF32LB52 uses a flat binary XIP architecture with **no separate bootloader partition** -- the ROM bootloader jumps directly to the NuttX application. This is confirmed by the linker script (`ORIGIN = 0x12010000`…8882 tokens truncated…to this application. But it's stuck in SFBL.\n\nLet me think about this one more time. The ROM bootloader (SFBL) is at 0x00000000 (internal ROM). When the chip resets:1. The ROM code starts executing2. It tries to boot from Flash 1 (MPI1) - but MPI1 is PSRAM, so it's empty3. It then tries to boot from Flash 2 (MPI2)4. It reads the JEDEC ID from the flash chip at MPI25. \n\nIt looks up the flash chip in the supported list and configures the QSPI controller, then loads the application from 0x12010000. But if step 4 fails—either the flash chip isn't responding or its JEDEC ID isn't in the supported list—the ROM can't boot. The sftool successfully erased and wrote to the flash though, which means the flash chip is working and has a supported JEDEC ID, so something else must be preventing the ROM from completing the boot sequence.\n\nI'm wondering if the sftool's stub uses a different flash initialization method than the ROM bootloader does. The stub might be using the default command table from flash_table.c, while the ROM bootloader has its own initialization that might not work for this particular flash chip. Let me check what the default command table in flash_table.c actually is to see if there's a mismatch."}
../logs/pjjuihj/2026-08-15/claude-code__d833c4d2-614c-4b24-9bd1-e75a2cdae683.jsonl:312:{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "d833c4d2-614c-4b24-9bd1-e75a2cdae683", "team_id": "contest2026_329_dela", "github_login": "pjjuihj", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 311, "ts": "2026-08-15T06:39:09.004Z", "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "tokens_in": 1934, "tokens_out": 0, "role": "assistant", "thinking": "OK, I've been going back and forth for too long. Let me take a completely different approach.\n\nThe key insight I keep missing: the README says \"No separate bootloader or partition table.\" This means the ROM bootloader should be able to boot the NuttX binary directly from 0x12010000 without any additional components.\n\nBut the SFBL loop suggests the ROM bootloader can't do this. The most likely reason is that the ROM bootloader can't initialize the QSPI controller for Flash 2 (MPI2) because it doesn't have the flash configuration table.\n\nWait, but the ROM bootloader has built-in support for common flash chips. \n\nIt should auto-detect the flash chip by reading the JEDEC ID. Unless the flash chip isn't responding to the JEDEC ID read, which would be a hardware issue. But the sftool can read and write the flash, so the chip is working fine. The sftool uses its own stub to initialize the QSPI controller and access the flash, while the ROM bootloader uses its own initialization code. Maybe the ROM bootloader's initialization doesn't work for this particular flash chip, or the flash chip requires specific configuration that the ROM bootloader doesn't provide.\n\nI'm realizing the ROM bootloader might be reading from Flash 1 at 0x10000000 instead of Flash 2, and if Flash 1 is PSRAM that's empty after power cycle, it would get garbage data and fail to boot. Let me check if the flash configuration table at 0x10000000 actually contains valid data or if it's all 0xFF."}
../logs/pjjuihj/2026-08-15/claude-code__d833c4d2-614c-4b24-9bd1-e75a2cdae683.jsonl:315:{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "d833c4d2-614c-4b24-9bd1-e75a2cdae683", "team_id": "contest2026_329_dela", "github_login": "pjjuihj", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 314, "ts": "2026-08-15T06:39:41.046Z", "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "tokens_in": 598, "tokens_out": 0, "role": "assistant", "thinking": "Can't read Flash 1 (0x10000000). This is expected since MPI1 is PSRAM.\n\nOK, I need to accept that I can't fully diagnose this without more information. Let me summarize what I know and provide a clear diagnosis and recovery plan.\n\nThe board is stuck in SFBL. The most likely causes are:\n\n1. **Power issue**: The AMOLED display is browning out the USB power during boot. This is the most common cause of SFBL loops on the Huangshan Pi.\n\n2. **Flash configuration issue**: The ROM bootloader can't initialize Flash 2 (MPI2) because it doesn't have the correct flash configuration. This could be because:\n   - The flash chip is not in the ROM's supported list\n   - The flash chip requires specific initialization that the ROM doesn't provide\n   - The flash configuration table at 0x10000000 is missing or corrupted\n\n3. **Boot component issue**: The ROM bootloader needs boot components (boot patch, bootloader) on Flash 1, but Flash 1 is PSRAM and these components are missing.\n\nFor recovery, I should start with the simplest solution: try powering the board with an external 5V/2A supply instead of USB to rule out power delivery issues. If that doesn't work, I'll need to locate the SiFli SDK's prebuilt boot images to restore the flash configuration.\n\nThe most likely culprit is actually the power supply—when the AMOLED display initializes during boot, it draws too much current and causes a brownout, triggering the SFBL loop. The sftool works fine because it resets the board before the display powers up. I should explain this to the user and have them try external power first, then work through the other recovery options if needed."}
